WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:12.640
Welcome to episode 600! And today you will see&nbsp;
your own rough drawings come to life as beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.520
photorealistic images. And it turns out, you can&nbsp;
try it too. I’ll tell you about it in a moment.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:27.280
This technique is called GauGAN2, and yes, this&nbsp;
is really happening. In goes a rough drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.760
and out comes an image of this quality. That&nbsp;
is incredible. But here, there is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:41.360
that is even more incredible. What is it? Well,&nbsp;
drawing is an iterative process. But, once we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.600
committed to an idea, we need to refine it over&nbsp;
and over, which takes quite a bit of time, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.480
let’s be honest here, sometimes, things come out&nbsp;
differently than we may have imagined. But this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:01.680
this is different. Here, you can change things&nbsp;
as quickly as you can think of the change. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.160
can even request a bunch of variations on&nbsp;
the same theme, and get them right away.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:16.240
But that’s not all, not even close. Get this, with&nbsp;
this, you can draw, even without drawing. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.120
really. How is that even possible?&nbsp;
Well, if we don’t feel like drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.560
we can just type what we wish to see,&nbsp;
and…my goodness, it not only generates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.320
these images according to the written description,&nbsp;
but this description can get pretty elaborate.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.880
For instance, we can get ocean waves, that’s&nbsp;
great, but now, let’s add some rocks…and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.280
a beach too. And there we go!

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.400
We can also use an image as a starting point,&nbsp;
then, just delete the undesirable parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:59.360
and have it inpainted by the algorithm. Now,&nbsp;
okay this is nothing new, computer graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.280
researchers were able to do this for more&nbsp;
than 10 years now. But hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.360
because they couldn’t do this. We can fill&nbsp;
in these gaps with a written description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.200
Couldn’t witness the northern lights&nbsp;
in person? No worries, here you go.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.360
And, wait a second…did you see that? There&nbsp;
are two cool really cool things to see here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.280
Thing number one, it even redraws&nbsp;
the reflections on the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.600
even if we haven’t highlighted that part for&nbsp;
inpainting. We don’t need to say anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:41.280
it will update the whole environment to reflect&nbsp;
the new changes by itself. That is amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.960
Now, I am a light transport researcher by&nbsp;
trade, and this makes me very, very happy.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.800
Thing number two, I don’t know if you caught this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.680
but this is so fast, it doesn’t&nbsp;
even wait for your full request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.320
it updates after every single keystroke. Look.&nbsp;
Drawing is an inherently iterative process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.320
and iterating with this is an absolute&nbsp;
breeze. Not will be a breeze. It is a breeze.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.080
Now, after nearly every Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
episode where we showcase an amazing paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:20.320
I get a question saying something like&nbsp;
“okay, but when do I get to see or use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.800
in the real world?”. And rightfully so, that&nbsp;
is a good question. The previous GauGAN paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.520
was published in 2019, and here we are, just&nbsp;
a bit more than 2 years later, and it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:40.400
transferred into a real product. Not only that,&nbsp;
but the resolution has improved a great deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.720
about 4 times of what it was before, plus&nbsp;
the new version also supports more materials.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.200
And we are at the point where this&nbsp;
is finally not just a cool tech demo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.080
but a tool that is useful for real&nbsp;
artists. What a time to be alive!

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.880
Now, I noted that earlier, I did not say that&nbsp;
iterating this will be a breeze. But it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:10.240
breeze. Why? Well, great news, because you&nbsp;
can try it right now in two different ways.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.160
One, it is now part of as&nbsp;
desktop application called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.960
NVIDIA Canvas. With this, you can even export the&nbsp;
layers to Photoshop and continue your work there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.960
This will require a relatively&nbsp;
recent NVIDIA graphics card.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.320
And two, there is a web app too that you&nbsp;
can try right now! The link is available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.640
in the video description, and if&nbsp;
you try it, please scroll down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.080
and make sure to read the instructions and&nbsp;
watch the tutorial video to not get lost.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.920
And remember, all this tech transfer from&nbsp;
paper to product took place in a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.640
of two years. Bravo NVIDIA! The pace of&nbsp;
progress in AI research is absolutely amazing.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

